{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>et the blessing etc.</b> The saying of <b>et</b> comes to awaken [the awareness] that besides the blessing that is grasped from the way of HaShem, there is further with it a promise of good, and that is <b>which you will hear</b>, for hearing in Torah is a wondrous delight and a reviver of the soul, as it says (Isaiah 55:3) ״שִׁמְעוּ וּתְחִי נַפְשְׁכֶם״, and one who feels the taste of Torah — his soul will tell us that upon him it is fitting to repay a good reward by giving a fully abundant gift, and it goes without saying that one cannot claim wages for it." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory regarding merit, that it has a principal [vestment] and it has fruits (Kiddushin 40a), and this is what he alluded to by saying <b>et the blessing</b>, for the word \"the blessing\" denotes a world that is the source of blessing, all of which is blessed, and the inclusion of את is for the fruits of the merit that one eats in this world." "And the verse is precise in saying <b>which</b>: the verse revealed that the prophet did not wish to cast the matter in terms of doubt, to say \"if,\" but said \"which\" — a matter of certainty, as the soul of the righteous hoped for them. And the verse suspended the blessing upon hearing, and did not mention either action or observance, because one who hears the words of the living Elohim — the Torah improves his soul and sets him straight, for it contains an elixir of life, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Lamentations Rabbah, Proem) — and this is their language: ״הַמָּאוֹר שֶׁבָּהּ מַחְזִירוֹ לְמוּטָב״ (the light within it returns him to the better). Therefore it suspended everything upon hearing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:40.666191564Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}